verses.text:
But yet the wicked are not so;
They are like chaff that blows away.
They will not in the judgment stand,
Nor sinners with the righteous stay.
The Lord the way of just men knows;
The wicked to destruction goes.

Give answer when I call, O God;
You are my righteousness.
To me be gracious; hear my prayer;
You freed me in distress.
You freed me in distress.

Know the Lord His saints has set apart in grace; 
And the Lord will hear me when I seek His face.
Tremble in your anger, yet from sin depart;
On your bed, in silence, speak within your heart.

Offer sacrifices that are right and just;
On the LORD relying, in Him place your trust.
“Who will show us goodness?” many people say;
The light of Your face, Lord, lift on us, we pray.

O LORD, my God, I take refuge in You;
O save me from all those who have pursued.
Lest they should tear me like lions with prey;
With none to rescue, they’d drag me away.

He whets His sword if one does not repent;
He has His bow already strung and bent.
His deadly weapons He has now prepared;
He makes His arrows fiery, deadly shafts.

You chastened the nations, the wicked destroyed;
Their names You erased and forever made void.
The foe is consumed and completely erased;
Their cities destroyed and their mem’ry effaced.

The Lord is a stronghold to help the oppressed;
A stronghold of safety in times of distress.
Those knowing Your name, Lord, their trust in You place;
You have not forsaken those seeking Your face.

Sing praise to the Lord, who in Zion does dwell;
Among all the peoples His great doings tell.
When blood He avenges, His mem’ry is clear;
The cry of the poor never fades from His ear.

Lord, see what I suffer from malice and hate; 
Have mercy! O lift me away from death’s gate;
In gates of the daughter of Zion I’ll praise,
Rejoicing in Your mighty power to save.

The nations are sunk in the pit they prepared;
Their foot in the net which they hid is ensnared.
The Lord by His judgment has made Himself known;
He catches the wicked in snares of their own.

Arise, Lord, that man may not make himself strong; 
Let nations be judged in Your presence for wrong.
O LORD, put Your fear and Your terror in them;
Let nations know truly that they are mere men.

Why do You stand so far away, O Lord?
Why do You hide Yourself in troubled times?
In pride the wicked chase and trap the poor;
Let them be caught in schemes they have devised.
The wicked boasts about his heart’s desires;
The greedy he commends, but spurns the LORD.

The wicked proudly does not choose to seek;
In all his thoughts he says, “There is no God.”
He cannot see Your judgments, yet he thrives;
He sneers with scorn at all his enemies.
He says within his heart “I’ll not be moved;
Through ev’ry age I will not suffer harm.”

He hides himself that he may seize the poor;
To seize the poor he traps him in his net.
He crouches, waiting for his chance to pounce;
He overpow’rs the helpless with his claws.
And so he thinks, “God has forgotten this;
He has concealed His face, He will not see.”

O break the arms of brutal, evil men;
Search out their wickedness till You find none.
The Lord is king through all eternity;
The Gentile nations perished from His land.

His eyes will surely see;
His eyelids test men’s sons.
The Lord tries just and wicked men;
His soul hates cruel ones.

His eyes will surely see;
His eyelids test men’s sons.
The Lord tries just and wicked men;
His soul hates cruel ones.

Help, Lord, because the godly man now ceases;
The faithful vanish from the sons of men.
They empty falsehood speak to one another;
With flatt’ring lips and double heart they speak.

But Your unfailing love I trust;
Your saving pow’r I praise.
The LORD in bounty dealt with me;
My songs to Him I raise.

But in Your lovingkindness
I have placed my confidence and trust;
My heart sings and my soul in salvation joys.
I will freely sing to the LORD,
Who has shown such goodness to me;
He has dealt so in bounty with my soul!

His nostrils smoked, His mouth blew fire;
Coals kindled in consuming flame.
Thick darkness was beneath His feet;
He bent the heavens when He came.

With men of kindness You are kind;
To blameless ones You’re blameless, too.
And with the pure You, too, are pure;
But with the crooked You are shrewd.

You gave me Your salvation’s shield;
I am upheld by Your right hand.
Your gentleness has made me great;
You clear my way, secure I stand.

I chased and overtook my foes;
I did not turn till they were slain.
I crushed them all beneath my feet;
They fell and could not rise again.

They cried for help, but none could save;
The LORD with silence met their shout.
I beat them fine as wind-blown dust;
Like refuse I have dumped them out.

The skies above declare the glory of our God;
The firmament displays His handiwork abroad.
From day to day they pour out speech;
Their knowledge every night they teach.

They’re more to be desired 
Than stores of finest gold;
Than honey from the comb 
More sweetness far they hold.
With warnings they Your servant guard;
In keeping them is great reward.

O let the words I speak 
Be pleasing in Your sight;
And may my inmost thoughts 
Be in Your judgment right.
O LORD, You are a Rock to me;
You have redeemed and set me free.

But still You are the Holy One;
On Isr’el’s praise You are enthroned.
Our fathers put their trust in You;
You saved them when to You they groaned.
To You they cried, and rescue came;
They trusted, and were free from shame.

But still You are the Holy One;
On Isr’el’s praise You are enthroned.
Our fathers put their trust in You;
You saved them when to You they groaned.
To You they cried, and rescue came;
They trusted, and were free from shame.

Like dogs, the wicked circle me;
And they have pierced my hands and feet.
I now can number all my bones;
With staring eyes they look at me.
My clothes among them they divide,
And for my robe with dice decide.

But be not far from me, O LORD;
Hurry, my Strength, send help to me!
My soul deliver from the sword;
From dogs and lions set me free.
Though by the ox I’m nearly torn,
Your answer saved me from its horn.

I’ll praise You in the gathering,
Telling my brothers of Your name;
All you who hold the LORD in awe,
To Him Your praises now proclaim.
Give Him the glory, Jacob’s seed;
All Isr’el’s sons, fear Him indeed!

Within the congregation great
I offer praise You have supplied;
I’ll pay my vows with those who fear.
With food the meek are satisfied;
Those seeking Him will praise the Lord.
May your heart live for evermore!

The LORD’s my Shepherd, leading me;
I will not be in need.
He’ll make me lie in pastures green;
By quiet waters lead.

The LORD is my Shepherd, lack nothing shall I.
In lush, verdant pastures He there makes me lie;
Beside tranquil waters He leads me along;
Because He restores me, my soul is made strong.

To You I lift my soul;
LORD God, in You I trust.
O do not let me suffer shame;
And let my foes not boast.
Yes, those who wait on You
No shame will ever see;
Let shame be theirs who without cause
Behave with treachery.

Instruct me in Your paths;
LORD, make me know Your way.
Teach me and make me follow You, 
That in Your truth I’ll stay;
Since You have been my God,
The One who rescues me,
For You I’m waiting all the day;
I wait expectantly.

LORD, vindicate me; I have walked 
In my integrity.
And I have trusted in the LORD;
I’ve been unwavering.
Examine me and prove me, LORD; 
Test heart and mind, I pray.
Since I behold Your steadfast love;
Your truth has led my way.

LORD, vindicate me; I have walked 
In my integrity.
And I have trusted in the LORD;
I’ve been unwavering.
Examine me and prove me, LORD; 
Test heart and mind, I pray.
Since I behold Your steadfast love;
Your truth has led my way.

The LORD is my light and my salvation; 
By whom am I dismayed?
The LORD is my life’s defense and stronghold; 
Of whom am I afraid?

The LORD’s voice in flashes of lightning exclaims,
The places it strikes all burst out into flames;
The voice of the LORD makes the wilderness quake;
The LORD makes the desert of Kadesh to shake.

The LORD’s voice in flashes of lightning exclaims,
The places it strikes all burst out into flames;
The voice of the LORD makes the wilderness quake;
The LORD makes the desert of Kadesh to shake.

In You, O LORD, I put my trust; 
Ashamed let me not be;
According to Your righteousness,
O do deliver me.

In You, Lord, I take refuge;
Let shame not come to me.
In righteousness give answer;
Set me at liberty.
O hasten to my rescue,
And to my plea give heed;
O be my rock and refuge,
A fortress in my need.

I say, “You are my God,” LORD;
With faith in You I stand.
Save me from persecutors;
My times are in Your hand.
O may light from Your presence
Upon Your servant shine;
And in Your lovingkindness
Make Your salvation mine.

I will at all times bless the LORD; 
His praise my mouth will always voice.
And in the LORD my soul shall boast;
The poor will hear it and rejoice.

Now men arise to bear false witness;
They ask me things I do not know.
My good they pay back with their evil;
It fills my heart with grief and woe.
When they were sick I dressed in sackcloth;
I fasted with humility;
But all the prayers I offered for them
Would keep returning back to me.

By the LORD the just are rescued; 
He’s their strength in troubled days. 
From the wicked He delivers; 
All who trust the LORD He saves.

Loathsome are my wounds neglected;
My own folly makes it so;
Bowed with pain, with grief dejected,
All day long I mourning go.

For my loins are filled with burning;
There’s no soundness in my flesh.
Feeble, crushed, I’m ever groaning;
In my spirit’s restlessness.

For I am about to stumble;
Grief has ever with me been.
I iniquity acknowledge;
Deeply troubled by my sin.

Silent, I ceased from speaking even good;
My heart within was hot, my sorrow stirred.
And while I mused a fire began to burn;
Then with my tongue I spoke this earnest word:

“LORD, make me know my end, my numbered days;
And let me know how fleeting is my life;
For You have made my days a handbreadth long;
My lifetime is as nothing in Your sight.

Each man at best is but a fleeting breath.
Man, like a shadow, wanders to and fro;
Surely an uproar he creates in vain;
He never knows to whom his riches go.”

With Your rebukes You chasten man for sin;
His beauty fades beneath the touch of death.
You, like a moth, consume his precious things;
Oh, surely every man is but a breath.

How blessed the man who guides the poor
By counsel strong and clear;
The Lord will surely rescue him
When evil days draw near.
The Lord will guard him in the land;
His life is blessed indeed;
Nor will You let him fall before
His adversaries’ greed.

As deer for streams, I pant for God;
I pant for You so longingly.
I thirst for God, the living God;
How long until God’s face I see?

My yearning soul turns to the past;
To keep the feast I led the crowd.
Up to God’s house we’d come at last;
With joyful thanks we’d shout aloud.

O God, we have heard of Your works with our ears;
Our fathers have told what You did in past years:
How nations were crushed and cast out by Your hand; 
You planted our fathers to live in the land.

No trust will I place in my sword or my bow;
We’ll boast in the God who saved us from the foe;
All those hating us You have brought down in shame,
And so we will ever give thanks to Your name.

A river makes God’s city glad,
The Most High’s holy place.
God is in her; she won’t be moved;
God helps at break of day.
When nations rage, then kingdoms fall;
But when He speaks, earth melts.
The LORD of Armies is with us;
Our rock is Jacob’s God.

A river makes God’s city glad,
The Most High’s holy place.
God is in her; she won’t be moved;
God helps at break of day.
When nations rage, then kingdoms fall;
But when He speaks, earth melts.
The LORD of Armies is with us;
Our rock is Jacob’s God.

For God is King of all the earth;
Sing praises with a skillful tone.
God rules the nations from on high;
God sits upon His holy throne.

The princes and the nobles of the nations now draw nigh;
As people of the God of Abraham, they unify;
For this world’s shields belong to God, He is exalted high!
 Praises sing! Sing your praises unto God! Sing His praise! 
Lift your voices and sing praises to our King!

When kings advanced to fight, they saw it;
Behold, it filled them with dismay;
And in their panic and amazement
They turned around and ran away.

Hear this, all earth’s nations, now;
All living men, give ear;
All together, low and high, 
Both rich and poor, now hear.
I’ll speak wisdom with my mouth, 
Insight weighed within my heart; 
I’ll to proverbs lend my ear, 
Solve riddles with the harp.

Let no fear disturb your peace
When one’s house and wealth increase;
All his glory death will end;
With him nothing will descend.

From my sins, O hide Your face;
My iniquities erase.
O my God, renew my heart,
And a spirit right impart.
Cast me not from You, I pray;
Nor Your Holy Spirit take.

Prosper Zion in Your grace;
Build Jerus’lem’s walls in place.
Then will sacrifices right,
Off’rings burnt bring You delight;
Then will men, their vows to pay, 
Bulls upon Your altar lay.

Each one has turned aside;
Corrupt have all become;
There’s no one who does what is good,
No, not a single one.

Once fearless, now they fear; 
God scatters all their bones;
And you will put your foes to shame - 
They’re God’s rejected ones.

By Your name, O God, now save me;
Grant me justice by Your strength.
To these words of mine give answer;
O my God, now hear my prayer.
Strangers have come up against me,
Even men of violence.
And they seek my life’s destruction;
God is not within their thoughts.

By Your name, O God, now save me;
Grant me justice by Your strength.
To these words of mine give answer;
O my God, now hear my prayer.
Strangers have come up against me,
Even men of violence.
And they seek my life’s destruction;
God is not within their thoughts.

Throughout each day they twist my words;
Their thoughts toward me are hate.
They meet, they lurk, they mark my steps;
To take my life, they wait.
Can wicked nations flee Your wrath?
O God, humiliate!

You have recorded all my ways,
All pathways that I took;
Within Your bottle placed my tears,
Are they not in Your book?
When I cry out then my foes flee;
I know God is for me.

O be exalted high, O God;
Above the heavens stand;
And let Your glory be above
All earth, both sea and land.

God, shatter all their teeth within their mouth;
Break out the fangs of lions young, O LORD.
Like running water let them flow away;
Let arrows which they aim be headless shafts.

The just is glad when he sees vengeance come;
And in the wicked’s blood he’ll wash his feet.
Men say, “The righteous surely have reward;
There surely is a God to judge on earth.”

Free me, my God, from all my enemies;
From those who rise against me, keep me safe.
Save me from those who work iniquity;
Deliver me from men who thirst for blood.

See how they lie in ambush for my life;
Fierce men have launched attacks against me now;
They quickly come, opposing me, O LORD,
For no offense nor sin - no guilt of mine.

But You, O LORD, will laugh at them in scorn; 
You scoff at all the nations in disdain.
I wait for You, because You are my strength;
Truly a mighty stronghold is my God.

Let sinful deeds and lies catch up with them,
Because of lies and curses that they speak.
In wrath consume that they may be no more;
Let all earth know that God in Jacob rules.
At night like dogs my enemies return;
They prowl for food and growl unsatisfied.

But as for me, I’ll sing about Your strength,
Each day with joy, sing of Your steadfast love;
For You have been my fortress, my defense,
My refuge when the times of trouble come.
To You I sing my praises, O my Strength;
For He is God my Fort, my God of love.

For, God, You have heard the vows that I made;
I’m heir now with all those fearing Your name.
The king’s life You’ll lengthen, prolonging his days;
His years will be many, from age unto age.

My cry and prayer, O God, attend;
For from the earth’s remotest end
To You I raise my cry;
I call when troubles fill my breast;
O lead me to the rock of rest
That higher is than I.

They plot to bring his glory down,
In lies they take delight;
And while they bless him with their mouths, 
Their hearts are filled with spite. 
My soul, find rest in God alone;
He’s given hope to me.
He’s my salvation, stronghold, rock;
Unshaken I shall be.

Praise awaits You, God, in Zion;
Vows to You will be fulfilled.
You’re the One who hears our praying;
To You all mankind will come.
Evil words have overwhelmed me,
But You cover all our sins.

All earth to God raise joyful song;
Sing forth the glory of His name.
To Him give praises glorious.
Tell God, “How awesome are Your deeds; 
Your great strength leads Your foes in haste 
To show You their submission.
All earth worships;
To You bowing, to You praising, 
To You singing.
To Your name they sing their praises.”

O come and see the works of God,
He’s awesome in the things He does
Before the eyes of mankind.
He turned the sea into dry land;
They through the river passed on foot;
We’ll there rejoice before Him.
He rules ever
In His might; He sees the nations,
His eyes watching;
Let none rise up in rebellion.

I’ll enter, then, into Your house,
I’ll bring burnt offerings with me;
To You I’ll make my pledges.
I made these with my lips and mouth;
I promised many offerings
When I was in great trouble.
I will offer
Burning sacrifice of fatlings,
With ram’s incense;
I’ll make bulls and goats an off’ring.

Come listen, all who rev’rence God;
I’ll tell what He has done for me:
I cried to Him and praised Him.
If in my heart I cherished sin,
The Lord would not have heeded me;
But truly God has listened;
He has heard me.
God be blessed because He has not 
Scorned my praying,
Nor His faithful love turned from me.

O God, let peoples praise You; 
Let all the peoples praise.
Let nations come rejoicing,
And songs of gladness raise;
For You will judge the peoples 
With perfect equity;
You will for all earth’s nations
Their guide and leader be.

O God, let peoples praise You; 
Let all the peoples praise.
The earth has brought its bounty 
Throughout its harvest days;
Since God, our God, will bless us; 
Yes, God will blessing send, 
That all the earth may fear Him 
To its remotest end.

O God, let peoples praise You; 
Let all the peoples praise.
Let nations come rejoicing,
And songs of gladness raise;
For You will judge the peoples 
With perfect equity;
You will for all earth’s nations
Their guide and leader be.

O God, let peoples praise You; 
Let all the peoples praise.
The earth has brought its bounty 
Throughout its harvest days;
Since God, our God, will bless us; 
Yes, God will blessing send, 
That all the earth may fear Him 
To its remotest end.

Let God arise, His enemies be scattered;
And from His presence let His foes take flight.
Drive them like smoke, 
Like melted wax;
So let the wicked perish from His sight.

But let the just be joyful in God’s presence;
With gladness let the righteous ones rejoice!
Sing unto God, 
And praise His name;
To Him who rides through deserts lift your voice.

As melting wax before a fire,
Before God let them die.
But let the righteous all exult;
In God let them have joy;
Let them shout loud for joy!

Save me from sinking down in miry waters; 
Deliver me from haters and deep floods.
Let not the flood of waters overwhelm me,
Let not the pit enclose and swallow me.
LORD, answer me, for good is Your love;
And in Your great compassion turn unto me.

O do not hide Your face from me, Your servant. 
I am distressed; O quickly answer me.
Draw near to me and grant my soul redemption;
Because of all my foes, O ransom me.
You know my shame, dishonor, disgrace;
My persecutors are well known unto You.

Scorn cut my heart, and sickness came upon me;
For sympathy I looked, but there was none.
And though I looked for comforters to help me;
Yet even then I found not one of them.
But they have given gall in my food;
They gave me vinegar to drink for my thirst.

The meek will see and then be glad;
Those seeking God revive!
Because the LORD will hear the poor;
He’ll pris’ners not despise.

Hasten, God, my rescue; help me quickly, LORD;
Frustrate those who hunt me; shame be their reward.
Turn back, confuse those who in my hurt delight;
Pay them back with shame who jeer “Aha” with spite.

Let all seeking You, with joy in You abide;
Saying for Your rescue, “God be magnified.”
God, I am needy; O come to me, I pray;
You’re my Help, my Savior; LORD, do not delay.

But I am poor and afflicted;
Hasten to me, O God!
You are my help and my Savior;
O LORD, do not delay.

In You, O LORD, I put my trust; 
Ashamed let me not be.
O save me in Your righteousness;
Give ear, and rescue me.

O be my rock, my dwelling place,
My constant safe resort;
For my salvation You’ve ordained;
You are my rock and fort.

Do not forsake me in old age;
When strength fails, leave me not.
For enemies against me speak;
To take my life, they plot.

They say, “God has forsaken him;
Come seize him, none will save.”
O God, do not be far from me;
God, hurry to my aid.

Their eyes are bulging with excess;
Their hearts are full of things they seek.
They scoff; they threaten to oppress.
Disdainful words they proudly speak.
Their mouth the heights of heaven raids;
Their tongue around the world parades.

As from a dream You, Lord, awake;
Then You their image will despise.
When pierced, with heart about to break,
When bitter thoughts within me rise,
I senseless was and blind within;
A beast before You I have been.

Yet constantly I am with You;
You’ve taken hold of my right hand.
Your counsel shows me what to do,
And guides me in the things I plan;
And afterward, so shall it be,
You will with honor welcome me.

O Lord, recount how foes insult;
How fools have scorned Your name.
Your turtledove give not to beasts;
Your meek ones’ lives regard
And not always forget.

God is truly known in Judah;
Great His name in Israel.
He has pitched His tent in Salem;
His house stands on Zion’s hill.
There He broke the flaming arrows,
There war’s shield and sword made still.

God is truly known in Judah;
Great His name in Israel.
He has pitched His tent in Salem;
His house stands on Zion’s hill.
There He broke the flaming arrows,
There war’s shield and sword made still.

He split the rocks and gave them drink,
As from great deeps below;
He from the rock brought running streams;
Like rivers, waters flowed.

It is burned and hewn in pieces;
By Your frown will they be destroyed.
Lay Your hand on him You favored,
Son of man that You raised in strength.
Then from You we will not wander;
Make us live; we will call Your name.
O Lord God of Armies, turn us back;
Shine Your face on us, we’ll be saved!

“Then open wide your mouth to Me; 
And it I’ll surely fill.
But to My voice they gave no heed;
My Isr’el spurned Me still.
I left them to their stubborn heart, 
To walk by their own will.”

Judge the needy and the orphan,
And the poor, distressed defend;
Free the destitute and needy;
Save them from the wicked’s hand.

These are they who have no knowledge,
To perceive no effort make;
They walk on in utter darkness;
All of earth’s foundations shake.

Judge the needy and the orphan,
And the poor, distressed defend;
Free the destitute and needy;
Save them from the wicked’s hand.

These are they who have no knowledge,
To perceive no effort make;
They walk on in utter darkness;
All of earth’s foundations shake.

Together they conspire in deadly earnest;
Against You they have made a covenant.
The Ishmaelites are there, the tents of Edom,
The men of Moab with the Hagarenes.
See Gebal, Amalek, with men of Ammon;
Behold Philistia and them of Tyre.
For Ashur too has come and joins their forces;
They are the power of the sons of Lot.

And look, O God, upon our Shield;
The face of Your Anointed view;
One day within Your courts will yield
More good than thousands without You.
I’d rather stand near my God’s house
Than dwell in tents of wickedness.

A sun and shield is God the LORD;
The LORD gives grace and glory too;
From blameless ones, no good withholds. 
Blessed, LORD of hosts, those trusting You.

My soul is full of anxious cares and gloom;
My weary life draws near the silent tomb.
I count as those that to the pit descend;
I’m like the man whose strength is at an end.

My former friends You have estranged from me;
Yes, their abhorrence You have made me be.
Shut up am I, imprisoned here must stay;
Through deep distress my eyes both waste away.

The heavens and the earth are Yours;
You founded everything - 
The north and south: and to Your name
Mount Tabor, Hermon sing.
Your arm and hand are full of strength;
Your right hand’s lifted high.
On right and justice rests Your throne;
Your love and truth draw nigh.

Night’s terrors will not cause alarm;
Nor in the day will arrows harm;
Nor sickness stalking in the night,
Nor plagues destroying in day’s light.

To him I will salvation show;
On him great honor I’ll bestow.
I’ll give to him abundant life;
He’ll see My rescue with delight.

All sinners grow like grass, 
The wicked multiply;
And yet they’ll be destroyed.
But, LORD, You stay on high.
Your foes, LORD, fall;
Your foes will perish, evil doers scattered all.

The floods lift up, O LORD;
The floods lift up their voice.
The floods are lifting up their waves;
They make a mighty noise.

The LORD’s a mighty God and King;
Above all gods He is.
The depths of earth are in His hand;
The mountain peaks are His.

O come and let us worship Him;
Let us with one accord
In presence of our Maker kneel,
And bow before the LORD;

O come and let us worship Him;
Let us with one accord
In presence of our Maker kneel,
In presence of our Maker kneel,
And bow before the LORD;

O come to the LORD, joyful songs let us sing;
Loud praise to our Rock of salvation we’ll bring!
With thanksgiving let us before Him appear;
Come sing joyful psalms, let us praise Him with cheer.

O sing a new song to the LORD;
All earth sing to the LORD.
Sing to the LORD, and bless His name;
“He saves!” each day proclaim. 
His glory to all nations show;
His deeds let peoples know.

The LORD reigns, let earth rejoice;
Let the many isles be glad.
Clouds and darkness cover Him;
On true justice rests His throne.
Fire goes on ahead of Him, 
Burning up His enemies.

O enter then His gates with praise;
Within His courts your thanks proclaim;
With grateful hearts your voices raise
To bless and magnify His name.

To this my prayer, O listen, LORD;
And let my cry for help reach You.
In time of grief, hide not Your face,
Incline to me Your list’ning ear;
And answer quickly when You hear.

Roused with compassion, You will stand;
Kindness to Zion You will show.
The time has come which You have planned;
Your gracious favor she will know,
Your gracious favor she will know.

The earth You founded long ago;
Your mighty hands the heavens made.
Yet they will die while You endure;
Like worn out clothes whose use is done,
So You will change them, every one.

For our frame He well remembers;
That we’re merely dust He knows.
As for man, like grass, his days are;
As the meadow’s flower, he grows.

The beams of Your courts in waters You laid;
On wings of the wind Your pathway You made.
The clouds are Your chariot; the winds do Your will;
The flames and the lightnings Your pleasure fulfill.

Your works, LORD, are many, created in wisdom;
The whole earth is filled with the things You have made.
Consider the ocean, so vast, filled with creatures:
The large and the tiny - too many to count.
The ships sail upon it, Leviathan lives there;
You made it to play in the depths of the sea.

Where ships sail the deep and sea monsters play;
These all look to You to give food each day.
Whatever You give them they gather for food;
When Your hand You open You fill them with good.

Forevermore may the LORD’s glory stand;
The LORD will enjoy the works of His hand.
He looks at the earth and it trembles in fear;
He touches the mountains, the smoke then appears.

O thank the LORD, call on His name;
His deeds declare to all.
O sing to Him, sing praise to Him;
His wondrous works recall.

God’s covenant stays forever secure;
He brings it to mind, so shall it endure.
His word to a thousand descendants shall stand;
Through all generations, so did He command.

The covenant He with Abraham made;
His oath He in turn to Isaac relayed;
To Jacob confirmed it, a certain decree;
To Isr’el, a covenant permanently.

With all our fathers we have sinned;
We have done wickedly.
Our fathers did not understand
Your signs in Egypt seen;
Though they Your acts of love forgot,
And at the Sea rebelled,
He saved them still, for His name’s sake;
His power they beheld.

Then to a city forth they went;
He led them on a pathway straight.
So let them to the LORD give thanks
For all His love and wonders great.
The thirsty soul He satisfies;
With good the hungry He supplies.

In trouble to the Lord they cried;
He saved them from distress;
He sent His word to make them whole
And lift from wretchedness.

They wandered through wilderness, desert and waste;
No city to live in was found.
They suffered from hunger, they suffered from thirst;
Their soul grew exhausted and weak.
And then in their trouble they cried to the LORD
Because of the peril they faced;
And out from the dangers and troubles they knew
He brought them to safety at last.

Yet some lived in darkness, in shadow of death,
As prisoners wretched and chained,
Because they rebelled against what God had said;
The Most High’s good counsel they spurned.
And so He has humbled their heart with hard toil;
They stumbled but no one would help.
Then they in their trouble cried out to the LORD;
He rescued them from their distress.

God spoke in holiness:
“I will exultant stand;
I’ll measure Succoth’s valley and
Apportion Shechem’s land.
For Gilead is Mine;
Mine are Manasseh’s fields;
Yes, Ephraim My helmet is;
My scepter Judah wields.”

